#!/usr/bin/env ruby
# frozen_string_literal: true

# NO PUBLISHED SCHEMA'S PROSE RESTATES THE SHAPE BESIDE IT (K-1517).
#
# ADR-0023 re-scoped a descriptor's `description` to SEMANTICS ONLY: it says
# what a verb does, how, and what the result MEANS, and it «must never carry a
# field list, a type, a required/optional marker, or a param name that the
# schema also carries». The reason is not tidiness — it is that a fact stated
# twice drifts, and the copy in prose is the one nothing checks. That ADR names
# the file this repository publishes as one of the surfaces that follows under
# rule 1, so the clause binds the DESCRIPTOR SCHEMA here and not only the demo
# initializers in the reference implementation; ADR-0032 then widened the clause
# to EVERY published schema's property descriptions, which is the scope of the
# ban below.
#
# WHY THIS GUARD IS IN *THIS* REPOSITORY, and it is a refusal on the other side
# rather than an omission on this one. `reference/bin/check-descriptor-prose`
# asks the identical question of Ruby: it walks the syntax tree and compares
# each property's `description` with the membership its own sibling `required:`
# array decides. It CANNOT reach a JSON Schema, and its header says so. The
# reference repository's copy of this file — `e2e/schemas/*.schema.json` — is
# VENDORED, held byte-identical to what this repository publishes by
# `bin/check-spec-schemas` over there, so a rule reaching it from that side
# would demand an edit that guard fails on: a permanently red tree with no
# legal repair. The fix belongs where the bytes are authored, which is here.
#
# ── WHAT IT READS, DERIVED AND NOT LISTED ───────────────────────────────────
#
# Every tracked `spec/schemas/**.json`, read off `git ls-files` at run time —
# the published schemas AND the example/rejected fixtures beside them, because
# a fixture is published too and a description in one is read the same way.
# Inside each document the walk is structural rather than keyed on a file name:
#
#   PROPERTY   a named entry of a `properties` object, carrying a string
#              `description`. Its MEMBERSHIP is decided by the sibling
#              `required` array — present in it means required, absent from it
#              (or no array at all) means optional. That decision is machine-
#              readable and sits three lines from the prose, which is exactly
#              why the prose repeating it is redundant rather than wrong.
#
# Nothing else is read: a `description` on a schema ROOT, on a `$defs` member,
# or inside a `oneOf` branch has no membership array beside it to restate, so
# it is outside the question this guard asks. That boundary is a construction,
# not a list — the same discipline `check-descriptor-prose` keeps one repo over,
# and the same one that left OpenAPI parameter objects unheld there until
# somebody planted a break in one (K-1521).
#
# ── THE BAN AND THE CENSUS, AND WHY THE LINE IS WHERE IT IS ─────────────────
#
# THE LINE IS THE RULING, NOT THE RESIDUE, and those were the same thing only
# for as long as there was a residue. Do not read a number out of this comment:
# the run prints its own SCOPE block and its own CENSUS TOTAL, derived on every
# invocation, and those are the figures to quote (K-1451's caveat, applied to a
# guard's header). What is durable is the shape of the argument.
#
# THE BAN COVERS THE WHOLE CORPUS, AND IT DID NOT ALWAYS (ADR-0032, K-1527).
# ADR-0023 decision 1 re-scoped a DESCRIPTOR's `description` to semantics and
# named the descriptor surfaces one by one; it said nothing about the schema for
# a JWT's claims, a discovery document or an RFC 9457 problem. So this file was
# built with the ban standing exactly where the ruling stood — one schema — and
# COUNTING the identical shape everywhere else, on the measured reason that a
# gate red on a standing backlog nobody has agreed to drain is a gate somebody
# switches off. There WAS such a backlog: nineteen bare labels across auth,
# binding, discovery, kyc, mandates, pow and problem.
#
# K-1525 drained all nineteen by hand, keeping every semantic clause, which left
# the ban narrow and the drained documents held by NOTHING — a census is printed
# and never red, and a class drained by hand grows back. ADR-0032 supplies the
# missing half: the semantics-only clause binds every published JSON Schema's
# property descriptions, because the reason ADR-0023 gave is about the STRUCTURE
# of a JSON Schema — the membership decision is machine-readable and sits three
# lines from the prose — and that is as true of a JWT claim as of a descriptor.
# So R1 now reads the whole corpus, and it was green on the day it widened.
#
#   R1  BAN — no property description ANYWHERE in the corpus carries a bare
#       optionality label. Red.
#   C1  CENSUS — every property description carrying an optionality-ish word
#       that is NOT a bare label, printed with the membership its own `required`
#       array decides, so a human can read the two side by side. Never red, and
#       it is the surface on which the next word gets adjudicated.
#
# THE BAN'S SCOPE IS THE CORPUS, DERIVED FROM `git ls-files` AND NOT LISTED, so
# a schema added tomorrow is held with no edit here. What the self-test asserts
# in place of the old exactly-one-schema arm is that the corpus is non-empty,
# that the walk finds property descriptions in it, and that it finds them in
# MORE THAN ONE FILE — a scope that collapses back to one document is the same
# silent narrowing this section is about.
#
# THE MARKER IS THE ONE THE REFERENCE GUARD MEASURED, character for character,
# because the two ask one question of two languages and a second opinion nobody
# reconciles is worse than none:
#
#   * the word set is the five bare labels, written as one alternation so a
#     marker deleted from it fails its own probe rather than passing silently;
#   * the boundary excludes `-`, `_` and digits, because `licence-required` is
#     a compound adjective and `setup_required` is a WIRE VALUE a description
#     quotes — Ruby's `\b` calls all three a boundary and reddened four correct
#     descriptions the first time this was written over there (K-1514);
#   * a marker that is the complement of a COPULA is a predicate about the
#     value («the offset IS REQUIRED»), not a label on the property;
#   * a marker taking an INFINITIVE complement is a predicate about an ACTION
#     («never required TO BE declared»), and a membership label never takes one
#     (K-1521).
#
# WHAT IT DOES NOT COVER, stated rather than implied:
#
#   * the OTHER ADR-0023 clauses — a description carrying a field list, a type
#     or a param name. Those need the input schema read alongside the prose and
#     are a different check; this one holds the membership marker only.
#   * the narrative surfaces. `specification.html`, `spec/protocol.md` and
#     `spec/descriptor-house-style.md` describe the descriptor in prose with no
#     `required` array beside them, so there is nothing here to compare against.
#   * `FORBIDDEN.`, which `spec/schemas/schema-descriptor.schema.json` uses on
#     `params` — a third kind of membership label, decided by the `not: {}`
#     beside it. It is COUNTED (the census word set carries it) and not banned,
#     because the reference guard's word set was measured against a live corpus
#     and this one has not been; adding a word to a ban on the strength of one
#     site is the guess this workspace keeps recording the cost of.
#
# Usage: bin/check-schema-prose [--list] [--self-test]

require "json"
require "open3"

ROOT = File.expand_path("..", __dir__)

KNOWN_FLAGS = ["--list", "--self-test"].freeze
unknown = ARGV.reject { |a| KNOWN_FLAGS.include?(a) }
unless unknown.empty?
  warn "check-schema-prose: unknown argument(s) #{unknown.join(' ')}; known: #{KNOWN_FLAGS.join(' ')}"
  exit 2
end
LIST      = ARGV.include?("--list")
SELF_TEST = ARGV.include?("--self-test")

# ── the markers, taken from reference/bin/check-descriptor-prose ─────────────
#
# EVERYTHING BETWEEN THE TWO MARKERS BELOW IS SHARED WITH THAT GUARD, which
# asks this question of Ruby. `reference/bin/check-descriptor-prose` HOLDS THE
# TWO BLOCKS EQUAL — its LOCKSTEP section reads this file when the two repos
# are checked out beside each other, compares every line of CODE between these
# markers, and fails naming the first line that differs. Comments are not
# compared, so each side keeps its own commentary.
#
# So: an edit inside these markers is an edit to BOTH FILES, and moving one of
# these constants out from between the markers is itself a failure over there —
# the block has to be closed under its own references.

# >>> SHARED VOCABULARY

LABEL_WORDS = %w[optionally optional required mandatory compulsory].freeze
EDGE_BEFORE = /(?<![-_\p{L}\p{N}])/
EDGE_AFTER  = /(?![-_\p{L}\p{N}])/
COPULA_RE   = /\b(?:is|are|am|be|been|being|was|were|isn't|aren't|wasn't|weren't|not)[ ]+\z/i
MARKER_RE   = /#{EDGE_BEFORE}(?:#{LABEL_WORDS.join('|')})#{EDGE_AFTER}/i
INFINITIVE_AFTER_RE = /\A[ ]+to[ ]+\p{L}/

# THE SLASH PREDICATE, CARRIED BY BOTH GUARDS. A marker glued to its OPPOSITE by
# a slash names the AXIS — «names, required/optional, types, enums, ranges» is a
# list of the things a JSON Schema declares, in ADR-0023 decision 2's own
# words — and an axis is never a label on the property carrying the sentence.
# The shared `EDGE` already treats `-` and `_` this way for the same reason; a
# `/` is the third compound joiner and it is a separate test rather than a fourth
# character in `EDGE`, so the extra question stays visible on both sides.
#
# `reference/bin/check-descriptor-prose` asks the identical question of Ruby, and
# the vocabulary above plus `label_marker` below are HELD character-identical in
# the two files by that guard's LOCKSTEP arm (K-1526) — it was hand-kept until
# then, and the two diverged by exactly this predicate for a day because nothing
# asked. What the arm cannot do is see this file when the repositories are not
# checked out beside each other: there it says NOT COMPARED and this guard's own
# CI, which clones this repo alone, is one such place.
#
# MEASURED rather than imagined: the shape is live once in this corpus
# (`schema-descriptor.schema.json`'s `input_schema`) and twice more in the
# reference repository, in the comments that document the descriptor API
# (`kiosk-server/lib/kiosk/server/{actions,queries}.rb`). Those two are Ruby
# COMMENTS rather than `description:` values, so the reference guard does not
# read them.
SLASH_BEFORE_RE = %r{/\z}
SLASH_AFTER_RE  = %r{\A/}

# The first bare LABEL in this text, or nil.
def label_marker(text)
  offset = 0
  while (m = MARKER_RE.match(text, offset))
    offset = m.end(0)
    before = text[0...m.begin(0)]
    after  = text[m.end(0)..].to_s
    next if before =~ COPULA_RE
    next if after  =~ INFINITIVE_AFTER_RE
    next if before =~ SLASH_BEFORE_RE || after =~ SLASH_AFTER_RE

    return m[0]
  end
  nil
end

# <<< SHARED VOCABULARY

# Everything that SPELLS optionality, banned or not. `forbidden` is here and
# not in `LABEL_WORDS` — see the header.
CENSUS_RE = /
  #{EDGE_BEFORE}(?:#{LABEL_WORDS.join('|')}|forbidden)#{EDGE_AFTER}
  | \bomit(?:s|ted|ting|ission)?\b
  | \brequires?\b
  | \bdefaults?[ ](?:to|is)\b
  | \b(?:may|can)[ ]be[ ](?:omitted|left[ ]out|skipped|absent)\b
/xi

# ── the corpus ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

Prop = Struct.new(:file, :pointer, :name, :required, :description)

def tracked_schemas(root)
  out, _err, st = Open3.capture3("git", "-C", root, "ls-files", "--", "spec/schemas/*.json")
  raise "git ls-files failed in #{root}" unless st.success?

  out.lines(chomp: true).sort
end

# Walk one parsed document and yield every PROPERTY — see the header for why a
# `description` with no `required` array beside it is not one.
def properties_in(node, file, pointer, out)
  case node
  when Hash
    props = node["properties"]
    if props.is_a?(Hash)
      req = node["required"].is_a?(Array) ? node["required"] : []
      props.each do |name, value|
        next unless value.is_a?(Hash) && value["description"].is_a?(String)

        out << Prop.new(file, "#{pointer}/properties/#{name}", name,
                        req.include?(name), value["description"])
      end
    end
    node.each { |k, v| properties_in(v, file, "#{pointer}/#{k}", out) }
  when Array
    node.each_with_index { |v, i| properties_in(v, file, "#{pointer}[#{i}]", out) }
  end
  out
end

def parse(root, rel)
  JSON.parse(File.read(File.join(root, rel), encoding: "UTF-8"))
rescue JSON::ParserError => e
  raise "#{rel} is not parseable JSON: #{e.message}"
end

def collect(root, files)
  files.flat_map { |rel| properties_in(parse(root, rel), rel, "", []) }
end

# ── the run ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

def run_check(root)
  files = tracked_schemas(root)
  if files.empty?
    puts "FAIL: the corpus is EMPTY — a ban over no files reports the same clean zero as an " \
         "obeyed one, forever."
    return 1
  end

  props = collect(root, files)
  read_in = props.map(&:file).uniq

  puts "SCOPE (derived from `git ls-files` and from the documents themselves, not listed)"
  puts "  tracked schemas   : #{files.size}"
  puts "  property descriptions with a membership decided beside them: #{props.size}"
  puts "  R1 (ban) reaches  : every one of them — #{read_in.size} file(s) carry at least one " \
       "(ADR-0032)"
  puts "  C1 (census) reads : the same set, printed and never red — optionality prose that is " \
       "NOT a bare"
  puts "                      label is the prose doing its job, and this is where the next " \
       "word is adjudicated"
  puts

  if LIST
    props.each { |p| puts "  #{p.file}#{p.pointer}  required=#{p.required}  #{p.description[0, 120]}" }
    return 0
  end

  if props.empty?
    puts "FAIL: R1's scope is EMPTY — #{files.size} tracked schema(s) and not one property " \
         "description with a `required` array beside it, so the ban reads nothing and would " \
         "report a clean zero forever. Either the walk stopped recognising a property or the " \
         "documents changed shape; either way this is a decision, not a pass."
    return 1
  end

  failed = false
  violations = props.filter_map do |p|
    m = label_marker(p.description)
    [p, m] if m
  end

  if violations.empty?
    puts "R1. no property description in any published schema carries a bare optionality " \
         "label — OK (#{props.size} property description(s) over #{read_in.size} file(s))"
  else
    failed = true
    puts "R1. FAIL — #{violations.size} property description(s) restate the membership their " \
         "own `required` array decides:"
    violations.each do |p, m|
      decided = p.required ? "IS in the `required` array" : "is NOT in the `required` array"
      puts "  #{p.file}#{p.pointer}"
      puts "    label   : #{m.inspect}"
      puts "    decided : `#{p.name}` #{decided} beside this prose"
      puts "    remedy  : drop the label and keep the semantics. ADR-0023: a description says " \
           "what the field MEANS; the `required` array says whether it is there."
    end
  end

  census = props.select { |p| p.description.match?(CENSUS_RE) }
  if census.empty?
    puts "C1. no property description anywhere in the corpus spells optionality — census empty " \
         "(#{props.size} property description(s))"
  else
    puts "C1. #{census.size} property description(s) spell optionality — CENSUS ONLY, never " \
         "red. Read it: a bare label («OPTIONAL human-readable detail») restates the " \
         "`required` array three lines up and wants deleting; a sentence about what the VALUE " \
         "means when present, or about what OMITTING it does, is the prose doing its job."
    census.group_by(&:file).sort.each do |file, rows|
      puts "  #{file}"
      rows.each do |p|
        flag = label_marker(p.description) ? "BARE LABEL" : "no bare label"
        puts "    WARN #{p.pointer}  required=#{p.required}  [#{flag}]"
        puts "         #{p.description[0, 150]}"
      end
    end
  end

  puts
  puts "CENSUS TOTAL (derived on this run; printed, never red): #{census.size} row(s) over " \
       "#{files.size} file(s), of which #{census.count { |p| label_marker(p.description) }} " \
       "carry a bare label"
  failed ? 1 : 0
end

# ── the self-test ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# The detector's probes and foils, as data rather than as a number typed into
# the summary line: that line said «6 foil(s)» while eight were listed two
# screens above it, which is the hand-kept count this file's own header tells a
# reader not to trust (K-1529).
DETECTOR_PROBES = [
  ["MID-STRING", "The delivery date. Optional; the shop picks one when omitted."],
  ["UPPERCASE at the head", "OPTIONAL. RFC 7638 thumbprint of the public key."],
  ["a parenthetical", "Advisory minimum client version (optional)."],
  ["a semicolon label", "REQUIRED; an expired attestation is rejected."],
  ["a label after a clause", "Spending cap; REQUIRED (a nil cap is rejected)."],
].freeze

DETECTOR_FOILS = [
  ["the COPULA complement", "The offset IS REQUIRED, and a bare local time is refused."],
  ["a HYPHENATED compound", "Licence-required vehicles are gated by the attestation."],
  ["an UNDERSCORE wire value", "The hint is the literal `setup_required.`"],
  ["an INFINITIVE complement", "Reserved: always accepted, never required to be declared."],
  ["the verb form", "The verifier requires a signature over the whole document."],
  ["plain semantics", "Unchanged if omitted; the address's own clock decides."],
  ["a SLASH compound naming the axis", "Inputs: names, required/optional, types, enums."],
  ["the same compound the other way round", "It declares optional/required per field."],
].freeze

def self_test(root)
  require "tmpdir"
  require "fileutils"
  failures = []
  arms = 0
  check = lambda do |name, ok|
    arms += 1
    puts(ok ? "  ok   #{name}" : "  FAIL #{name}")
    failures << name unless ok
  end

  # ── the detector, on strings: one probe per marker and per historical
  #    spelling, and one foil per shape the boundary deliberately keeps out ──
  LABEL_WORDS.each do |w|
    check.call("R1's detector fires on the bare label #{w.inspect} — one probe per marker, so " \
               "a word deleted from the alternation fails here rather than passing silently",
               !label_marker("#{w.capitalize} thing an assistant may send.").nil?)
  end
  DETECTOR_PROBES.each do |name, text|
    check.call("R1's detector fires on #{name}", !label_marker(text).nil?)
  end
  DETECTOR_FOILS.each do |name, text|
    check.call("R1's detector does NOT fire on #{name} — a false red here is a repair that " \
               "makes the document worse", label_marker(text).nil?)
  end

  # ── planted breaks on disk, in a throwaway copy of the real corpus ────────
  Dir.mktmpdir("schema-prose-selftest") do |dir|
    FileUtils.mkdir_p(File.join(dir, "spec", "schemas"))
    # A fixture repository, so the plant never touches the working tree.
    Open3.capture3("git", "-C", dir, "init", "-q")
    files = tracked_schemas(root)
    files.each do |rel|
      dest = File.join(dir, rel)
      FileUtils.mkdir_p(File.dirname(dest))
      FileUtils.cp(File.join(root, rel), dest)
    end
    Open3.capture3("git", "-C", dir, "add", "-A")

    # Prefix the label onto the FIRST property description this document
    # carries, found by the same structural walk the run uses — no pointer and
    # no property name is written down here, so a plant cannot rot into a
    # no-op against a renamed field. It returns the name it hit, and a nil is
    # itself a failure.
    plant = lambda do |rel, label|
      path = File.join(dir, rel)
      doc  = JSON.parse(File.read(path))
      hit  = nil
      walk = lambda do |node|
        return if hit

        case node
        when Hash
          entries = node["properties"]
          if entries.is_a?(Hash)
            entries.each do |name, value|
              next unless value.is_a?(Hash) && value["description"].is_a?(String)

              value["description"] = "#{label} #{value['description']}"
              hit = "#{name} in #{rel}"
              break
            end
          end
          node.each_value { |v| walk.call(v) } unless hit
        when Array
          node.each { |v| walk.call(v) }
        end
      end
      walk.call(doc)
      File.write(path, JSON.pretty_generate(doc)) if hit
      hit
    end
    restore = ->(rel) { FileUtils.cp(File.join(root, rel), File.join(dir, rel)) }

    # WHICH FILES ARE PLANTED IN IS DERIVED, because the ban's scope is now the
    # whole corpus (ADR-0032) and a plant in one document would prove the rule
    # only for that document. First, middle and last of the files that carry a
    # property description, so the set spans the published schemas and the
    # example fixtures beside them and follows the corpus as it grows.
    with_props = collect(dir, tracked_schemas(dir)).map(&:file).uniq.sort
    check.call("the fixture corpus carries property descriptions in more than one file " \
               "(#{with_props.size}) — a plant set of one document proves the rule for one " \
               "document", with_props.size > 1)
    targets = [with_props.first, with_props[with_props.size / 2], with_props.last].compact.uniq

    targets.zip(["REQUIRED.", "OPTIONAL", "Mandatory —"].cycle.first(targets.size))
           .each do |rel, label|
      hit = plant.call(rel, label)
      if hit.nil?
        check.call("planting #{label.inspect} in #{rel}: it carries no property description " \
                   "any more, so this arm proves nothing", false)
        next
      end
      code = run_in(dir)
      restore.call(rel)
      check.call("R1 is RED on a #{label.inspect} label planted on `#{hit}`", code == 1)
    end

    check.call("…and GREEN again once the plants are restored", run_in(dir).zero?)

    # ── VACUITY: a corpus with no files, and a corpus whose files carry no
    #    property description, are FAILURES rather than passes — a check over
    #    nothing reports the same clean zero as an obeyed one ───────────────
    Dir.mktmpdir("schema-prose-empty") do |empty|
      Open3.capture3("git", "-C", empty, "init", "-q")
      check.call("an EMPTY corpus is red, not clean", run_in(empty) == 1)
    end

    Dir.mktmpdir("schema-prose-noprops") do |noprops|
      Open3.capture3("git", "-C", noprops, "init", "-q")
      FileUtils.mkdir_p(File.join(noprops, "spec", "schemas"))
      File.write(File.join(noprops, "spec", "schemas", "shapeless.schema.json"),
                 JSON.pretty_generate({ "$schema" => "https://json-schema.org/draft/2020-12/schema",
                                        "title" => "no properties anywhere",
                                        "type" => "string" }))
      Open3.capture3("git", "-C", noprops, "add", "-A")
      check.call("a corpus with schemas but NO property description is red — the ban's subject " \
                 "draining to nothing is a decision, not a pass", run_in(noprops) == 1)
    end
  end

  # ── VACUITY: the census must still have a live subject in this tree ───────
  files = tracked_schemas(root)
  props = collect(root, files)
  check.call("the corpus is non-empty (#{files.size} tracked schema(s))", !files.empty?)
  check.call("the walk finds property descriptions to read (#{props.size})", !props.empty?)
  spelled = props.count { |p| p.description.match?(CENSUS_RE) }
  check.call("C1's pattern still matches optionality prose in this repository (#{spelled} " \
             "row(s)) — a census that structurally cannot print a row is the same green " \
             "nothing as a rotted ban", spelled.positive?)
  # The old arm here required R1's scope to resolve to EXACTLY ONE schema, and
  # ADR-0032 retired the question. What replaces it is the property that scope
  # now has to keep: the ban reads MORE THAN ONE document, so a corpus that
  # quietly collapses back onto the descriptor schema — a `git ls-files`
  # pathspec that stops matching, a walk that stops recognising a property — is
  # a failure and not a clean run.
  read_in = props.map(&:file).uniq
  check.call("R1 reads property descriptions in more than one file (#{read_in.size}) — a scope " \
             "that collapses back to a single document is the narrowing ADR-0032 undid, " \
             "arriving silently", read_in.size > 1)

  if failures.empty?
    puts "SELF-TEST OK — #{arms} arm(s): #{LABEL_WORDS.size + DETECTOR_PROBES.size} detector " \
         "probe(s), #{DETECTOR_FOILS.size} foil(s), a label planted on disk in each of the " \
         "files named above, and the vacuity arms that refuse an empty corpus, a corpus with " \
         "no property description in it, and a scope collapsed onto one document"
    0
  else
    puts "SELF-TEST FAILED: #{failures.size} arm(s)"
    1
  end
end

# Run THIS script, unmodified, against another root. A copy would drift and a
# symlink would resolve back to the real one, so the file is invoked by path
# with the root passed in the environment.
def run_in(dir)
  _out, _err, st = Open3.capture3({ "KIOSK_SCHEMA_PROSE_ROOT" => dir }, RbConfig.ruby, __FILE__)
  st.exitstatus
end

require "rbconfig"

FIXTURE_ROOT = ENV.fetch("KIOSK_SCHEMA_PROSE_ROOT", nil)
EFFECTIVE_ROOT = FIXTURE_ROOT ? File.expand_path(FIXTURE_ROOT) : ROOT
puts "NOTE — running against FIXTURE ROOT #{EFFECTIVE_ROOT}, not this repository." if FIXTURE_ROOT

exit(SELF_TEST ? self_test(EFFECTIVE_ROOT) : run_check(EFFECTIVE_ROOT))
